WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Getting a budget alert

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:03.540
when your Google Cloud Platform
costs exceed your budget

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.300
is a great way to stay on
top of your cloud costs,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.250
but cost controls are
more about [? than ?]

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.143
just avoiding billing surprises.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:12.060
It's also important to
provide the flexibility

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:16.440
you need to scale and have a way
to restrict unnecessary costs.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.040
On this episode of
"Beyond Your Bill,"

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.707
we'll talk about how
programmatic budget

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:21.720
notifications can be
added to your budgets

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.240
and give some examples for
setting up advanced cost

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.332
controls.

00:00:25.332 --> 00:00:28.776
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.718 --> 00:00:34.760
In our previous video, we
went through setting up

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.010
budgets and alerts
in the GCP console

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.077
which trigger email
notifications.

00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:41.660
Since budget alerts are sent to
billing account administrators

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.970
and billing account
users, you can easily

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.310
control who gets
those notifications.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.707
However, that means you may
need to wait for those people

00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:50.540
to talk to the right
teams and investigative

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.460
if changes need to be made.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.070
If you want to put more
proactive controls in place,

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.660
programmatic budget
notifications

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.600
can allow you to run code
based on your budgets.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.120
After choosing a threshold
when setting up a budget,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.129
you can connect a Cloud
Pub/Sub to the budget

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:06.800
where you'll choose a
project and a Pub/Sub topic

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.750
to receive notifications.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.730
Once the budget is
published in the Pub/Sub,

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.320
you can have any
number of subscribers

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.540
that will handle the
notifications such as Cloud

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.580
Functions for our example.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.080
If you're not
familiar with Pub/Sub,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.090
think of it as a
simple message handler

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.160
which passes on any
events such as budget

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.520
updates to any services
that are subscribed to it.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.860
Cloud Functions is a
lightweight way and serverless

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.160
way to run code, and it's an
ideal solution for quickly

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.870
handling budget alerts.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.768
Any budgets you create
with a Pub/Sub topic

00:01:34.768 --> 00:01:36.560
will publish the current
status of a budget

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.500
multiple times per day rather
than waiting for a threshold

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.910
to be crossed.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.440
With each budget
you set up, you can

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.250
have both email notifications
and programmatic notifications.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.500
When setting up advanced
cost controls like this one,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.250
you'll want to make
sure that your finance

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.400
teams and your IT
teams work together

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.637
since this process
involves both sides.

00:01:54.637 --> 00:01:56.720
Once you've got everything
connected with Pub/Sub,

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.580
the Cloud Function will
receive notifications

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.038
with some important information
such as the billing account

00:02:01.038 --> 00:02:03.380
ID, the display
name of the budget,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.390
the most recent
threshold exceeded,

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.945
and the amount of your costs
for the scope of the budget.

00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:10.070
Now that you're writing
code with this information,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.470
you can pretty much do anything
the Google Cloud Platform

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.330
API will let you do.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.340
One very common example
is to post an update

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.740
to Slack or other
platforms, connecting

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.890
any tools your team uses to
stay on top of your costs.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:24.090
Again, this keeps
people up to date,

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.630
but we can also automate
direct cost controls

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.670
to respond to these
notifications.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.680
One of the more drastic
actions you can take

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.650
is to disable billing
for a project entirely.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.590
For example, you may have a
development or test environment

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.810
and a dev team that
constantly spins up

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.800
resources in that environment.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.220
If someone on the team spins
up an expensive resource

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.080
like a large compute
instance and then

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:49.080
forgets to turn it off, it can
rack up costs pretty quickly.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.540
One way to avoid
this may be to set

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.600
a budget and a programmatic
budget notification that

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.700
removes the billing
account from the project

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.917
after the specific
cost is reached.

00:02:57.917 --> 00:02:59.750
It's important to note
that when you disable

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.150
billing on a project, all
the billable resources

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.723
for that project are shut
down and then deleted.

00:03:04.723 --> 00:03:06.140
This is probably
not something you

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.700
want to do in a
production environment,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.290
but it can be a quick
way to make sure

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.660
that you set a very hard
limit for your costs

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.040
on that specified project.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:15.650
Sometimes a more
nuanced approach

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.170
is better, such as limiting
the number of resources

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.320
that can be spun up with
quota, or simply stopping

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.780
all the compute instances
without deleting them.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.480
That way you're not paying for
them and the appropriate team

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.177
can investigate why the usage
was higher than planned.

00:03:29.177 --> 00:03:31.760
There's some sample code in the
links below for sending alerts

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.580
to Slack, disabling
billing for a project,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.800
and for stopping all
compute instances when you

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.417
hit a specified budget amount.

00:03:38.417 --> 00:03:40.250
These are great starting
points, and they're

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.170
easy to extend with just
a little bit of code

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.970
to fit your business needs.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.080
When testing your code, you can
manually send Pub/Sub messages

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.622
so that you don't actually have
to wait for the budget amount

00:03:49.622 --> 00:03:50.510
to be triggered.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.030
Another driver of unnecessary
or accidental costs

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.240
can be from resources that
aren't being used optimally.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.130
Over-provisioned
resources are larger

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.380
than they need to be to serve
their current workloads.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.200
And since this is the cloud,
it can be easier to scale them

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.580
down as needed.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.710
Idle or part-time
resources can also

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.583
be an easy target
for saving costs

00:04:08.583 --> 00:04:10.250
since they can be
stopped or scaled down

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.170
when they're not at peak usage.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.515
This can also be automated
on a scheduled basis

00:04:14.515 --> 00:04:16.640
such as regularly looking
for resources that aren't

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.470
being used and removing them.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.660
Two great examples of
this are linked below.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.910
Both walk through
taking advanced actions

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.900
such as removing unused IP
addresses and attached disks,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.510
moving storage buckets to
less expensive storage classes

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:31.880
based on their
usage, and setting up

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.150
a schedule to start and stop
Compute Engine instances.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.980
Using programmatic
budget notifications

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.110
is another step towards
being able to setup cost

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.570
controls that match with
your business needs.

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.828
Each business is different,
so the specifics depend

00:04:43.828 --> 00:04:46.370
on the amount you're looking to
automate and extend your cost

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.640
controls, but the links below
give you a great starting point

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.267
and provide some sample code.

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:52.850
Check them out and
get a better handle

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:54.680
on controlling your cloud costs.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

